### NOT AN HTTP REQUEST — building a pack for a MISSING date by hand
###
### RECORDED, NOT EXECUTABLE. REST Client would try to parse the shell lines below as a request, so
### they stay inside this comment. Run them in a terminal.
###
### This is the README bootstrap invoke, and it is how a missing date gets a pack by hand -- a fresh
### stack serves an empty app until the next 03:33 UTC and nothing tells you, because
### createPackHandler returns normally when there is nothing to do and the level="ERROR" log
### subscription therefore fires nothing.
###
### template.yaml sets no FunctionName on CreatePackFunction, so CloudFormation generates one with a
### stack suffix -- `lull-api-CreatePackFunction-<suffix>`. A bare `--function-name
### lull-api-CreatePackFunction` returns ResourceNotFoundException. Look the real name up first:
###
###   FN=$(aws cloudformation describe-stack-resource --stack-name lull-api \
###     --logical-resource-id CreatePackFunction \
###     --query 'StackResourceDetail.PhysicalResourceId' --output text)
###
###   for d in $(date -u +%F) $(date -u -v+1d +%F); do
###     aws lambda invoke --function-name "$FN" \
###       --payload "$(printf '{"date":"%s"}' "$d")" --cli-binary-format raw-in-base64-out \
###       /dev/stdout
###   done
###
### It TOPS UP; it never replaces. createPack generates only the difficulties a pack is missing, so
### re-running it against a date that already has three goFigures adds nothing and is safe to repeat.
### It builds the self-contained puzzles and hands everything that needs a model call to the async
### builders, through the one fan-out in services/lambda.ts.
###
### That is SIMILAR to the request path, not identical: createPack runs every self-contained
### generator with no time budget, while the GET path (fillPack) filters on `inRequest` and applies
### the 10-second budget. Both are in services/packs.ts. They coincide only because goFigure is the
### sole self-contained generator today.
###
### That also means it is NOT a way to rebuild a pack that already exists. Rebuilding is a delete
### followed by this, in order:
###
###   0. SHIP lull-ui's metadata reader FIRST. GoFigureHintMetadata gains a required field, and this
###      API must not emit a shape the client has not learned. Not optional and not reorderable.
###   1. Delete every pack item from the packs table by hand. Nothing after this point may read a
###      puzzle built before the shape change. Nothing automates it and nothing ever will by
###      accident: the table carries Retain on both delete and replace, createPack tops up rather
###      than replaces, and -- AS OF STEP 2 AND NOT BEFORE -- no Lambda role holds a delete action
###      on it. Until that deploy lands the third clause is a PLAN rather than a fact, because the
###      grant removal is on this branch and not on master, and the operator running step 1 in the
###      pre-merge window should know which of the two it is standing in.
###   2. Deploy lull-api.
###   3. Re-run the bootstrap invoke above for EVERY DATE from PACK_START_DATE through tomorrow,
###      serially. NOT "today and tomorrow", which is what this runbook said for a smaller change.
###      Step 1 empties the table and isValidPackDate accepts 2026-08-01 through tomorrow, so
###      bootstrapping two dates leaves three weeks of servable archive dates holding ZERO puzzles,
###      with every contribution applying to every one of them. A cold client's prefetch then walks
###      eight dates, finds eight empty packs, and takes a claim plus a builder fan-out on each: the
###      MAXIMUM possible fan-out, arriving at the exact moment the fan-out was reduced.
###      COUNT THE DATES ON THE DAY rather than trusting a number written here; the arithmetic is
###      (tomorrow - PACK_START_DATE) + 1, inclusive of both ends. On 2026-08-23 that is 2026-08-01
###      through 2026-08-24 -- 24 dates. Priced rather than waved at: one createPack each, which
###      makes no model call, plus one phrase build each, which makes two (generatePhrases and
###      reviewPhrases). So ~48 Opus calls, serial, attended, one time. modelGenerators ships empty,
###      so the model builder adds nothing. That is the same spend the alternative incurs; the
###      difference is that this version is bounded, ordered and watched, and the alternative is
###      triggered by whoever opens the app first.
###
###      The loop above stops at tomorrow. Widen it, keeping it serial:
###
###        for d in $(node -e 'const s=Date.UTC(2026,7,1),e=Date.now()+86400000;
###          for(let t=s;t<=e;t+=86400000)console.log(new Date(t).toISOString().slice(0,10))'); do
###          aws lambda invoke --function-name "$FN" \
###            --payload "$(printf '{"date":"%s"}' "$d")" --cli-binary-format raw-in-base64-out \
###            /dev/stdout
###        done
###
###   4. Fetch each live pack date and assert `kind` is present on every goFigure rung. Skipping this
###      leaves a runtime `undefined` behind a non-optional field -- the exact case read sites were
###      told not to branch on.
###   5. `npm run audit-hints -- lull-api-packs --days 2` to confirm the audit still reads the new
###      shape. THE TABLE NAME IS REQUIRED; a bare run reads the test table, and a clean audit of the
###      test table says nothing about production.
###   6. Confirm the new alarms in the logs, and confirm THE FILTER READS THEM. After the first
###      nightly run, check that `stopReason` appears on the model-invocation lines and that no
###      max_tokens ERROR fired -- that is the only reading anyone gets on whether 32,000 tokens was
###      enough. IN THE SAME PASS, confirm that a real logError line actually matches
###      '[timestamp, uuid, level="ERROR", message]'. That filter is POSITIONAL, not a substring
###      search; four subscriptions in template.yaml use it and three shipping conditions on this
###      branch assume it works (the per-type logError({ type }) on both lanes, bestEffort's
###      type-level line, and the stop_reason promotion); and nothing in this repo has ever checked
###      that logError's emitted shape satisfies it. One log-group query.
###
### Nothing else is migrated and nothing backfills content. A PLAYED DAY DOES GET A REBALANCED PACK,
### and that is deliberate: the argument for leaving played days alone holds while the archive still
### HAS those packs, and does not survive step 1 deleting them. The choice is then between a rebuilt
### pack and no pack at all, and no pack is what the fan-out feeds on. Player progress is keyed to
### puzzle ids step 1 already destroyed, so nothing extra is lost by rebuilding.
###
### One visible consequence, named so nobody debugs it as a bug: after every deploy, each cached pack
### in the client's retention window reports incomplete once and the window refetches. That is the
### designed behaviour. What has changed is that the ARCHIVE-WIDE version of it no longer drags a
### fan-out of model calls behind it -- availableFrom sees to that -- and after step 3 there should be
### no genuinely short date left to fan out at all.
###
### MERGING THIS BRANCH DEPLOYS IT, AND NOBODY IS ASKED FIRST. .github/workflows/pipeline.yaml is
### `on: push` (:17), and `deploy-prod` is gated on `github.ref == 'refs/heads/master'` and
### `needs: [deploy-testing]` (:241-242) and nothing else -- there is no `environment:` key anywhere
### in that file, so no manual approval and no protection rule stands between a merge and production.
### That fixes the ORDER: STEP 1 MUST BE RUN BY HAND IN THE WINDOW BEFORE THE MERGE. Step 2 is then
### the pipeline doing it on its own, not an operator picking a moment.
###
### Note the asymmetry, because the paragraph below is easy to read as covering both: the DELETE is
### not automated and never will be, while the DEPLOY is fully automated and cannot be held back.
###
### WHAT STEP 1 COSTS, stated honestly because two comments in this repo disagree about it. This
### block treats the delete as a routine precondition that step 3 undoes. The PacksTable comment in
### template.yaml says the opposite about the same table -- a deleted historical pack "is not
### guaranteed to come back identical, and the ids a player's progress is keyed to would not survive
### it", and "a stack delete or an accidental table replacement would destroy the entire archive with
### no way back" -- which is why that table carries Retain on both delete and replace. Step 1 is a
### hand-run version of exactly that destruction: it removes the ENTIRE archive, not only the dates
### step 3 rebuilds, and every puzzle id in it is regenerated rather than restored. (No line numbers:
### the block has moved once already and a stale citation reads as a checked one. Search PacksTable.)
###
### THE PRICE, PRICED. The archive is at most (today - PACK_START_DATE) + 1 days deep -- 23 days on
### 2026-08-23 -- and this is pre-launch. So step 1 costs regenerated historical puzzles, which are
### different puzzles rather than lost ones, plus any client-side lull:progress keyed to the old ids,
### which is orphaned rather than migrated. That is cheap NOW and expensive after launch, and it is
### the argument for landing the wire change early rather than deferring it behind a game branch.
### The price is accepted; what is not negotiable is the ORDER, because step 1 cannot be undone once
### step 2 has auto-deployed.
###
### NOTHING AUTOMATES THIS, and nothing ever will by accident. template.yaml sets DeletionPolicy and
### UpdateReplacePolicy to Retain on the packs table; no Lambda role holds a delete action on it --
### every role is a hand-scoped statement, none is a managed policy template -- AS OF THE DEPLOY IN
### STEP 2 AND NOT BEFORE, since the grant removal is on this branch and production is running
### master; and createPack tops up rather than replaces, so a pack written under an old shape
### survives every deploy, indefinitely, serving old-shape puzzles to a client built for new ones.
###
### NO SHIPPING CHANGE REQUIRES THIS RUNBOOK RIGHT NOW, and the paragraphs that said one did have
### been deleted rather than left standing -- they were about the hint-shape change, which has been
### executed. A runbook kept warm across branches that do not need it is a runbook the next branch
### skips, having read it three times and seen it matter none of them.
###
### WHICH KIND OF CHANGE YOU HAVE, since that is the whole of what decides it:
###
###   (a) AN ADDITIVE TYPE -- a new PuzzleType literal, a new HintMetadata member, a new `data` shape,
###       and no change to a shape already on the wire. Ship the lull-ui reader first, set the type's
###       availableFrom to the day after that ships, merge. NO WIPE: every stored pack keeps exactly
###       the puzzles it has, and a date before availableFrom never owes the new type. Themed
###       Anagrams is this case, and so are the two types after it.
###
###   (b) A SHAPE CHANGE TO AN EXISTING TYPE -- any field added, removed or retyped on a `data` or a
###       `hints` payload that stored packs already carry. Steps 0-6 above, in order, before release.
###       Step 0 is lull-ui's reader and it is not reorderable, and step 1 must be run BY HAND in the
###       window before the merge, because the merge auto-deploys.
###
### THE DETECTION STORY IS STILL THIN, and that is why (b) is a runbook rather than a deploy. Nothing
### validates a stored pack on read -- services/dynamodb.ts casts the parsed JSON straight to `Pack` --
### and no script, CI step or smoke check inspects `kind` anywhere. A stale-shape pack is served
### silently and the first symptom appears in lull-ui.
