### REFERENCE — PUZZLE DATA SHAPES
###
### RECORDED, NOT EXECUTABLE. Everything below is the `data` payload of the three puzzle types, as
### returned by GET /v1/packs/{date} above. Read once before writing a client.
###
### THE HINT LADDER, WHICH IS THE SAME SHAPE ON EVERY PUZZLE TYPE
###
### Every puzzle carries `hints`: exactly three rungs, ORDERED BY THE BACKEND, and every rung is
###
###   { "metadata"?: { ... }, "text": "..." }
###
### RENDER THEM IN THE ORDER THEY ARRIVE. Do not sort them and do not renumber them. The order is NOT
### always least-to-most-revealing: the phrase types do run that way, but a goFigure puzzle with a
### unique operator tuple deliberately spends rung 1 on op2, so its slots arrive 1, 0, 2. A client
### that re-sorts on an assumed ordering gets those puzzles wrong.
###
### `text` is the sentence, and it is RENDERED VERBATIM -- do not rewrite it, do not compose your own
### from the fields beside it, and do not translate it. For goFigure the backend writes it from a
### closed set of templates. For the phrase types it is model-written prose that passed the content
### gates in utils/phrase-checks.ts (the utils/charged-terms.ts blocklist, answer-leak check,
### 200-character cap) -- decided here,
### but not synthesized here, and worth knowing if you render into anything other than a text node.
### `metadata` is
### machine-readable structure for the board, present only where the type has any: goFigure has it,
### Missing Vowels and Cryptogram do not. A renderer that only prints `hint.text` works on all three
### types today and on every type added later.
###
### This is a BREAKING CHANGE from the shape before it, where phrase puzzles shipped three plain
### strings and goFigure shipped three field-only objects with no sentence at all. There is no
### transitional tolerance and no version flag: every pack item is deleted by hand before this
### deploys (the rebuild runbook above), so every pack served by this API carries the shape below.
###
### EVERYTHING ABOVE THIS LINE IS TRUE OF EVERY TYPE. Everything below sits inside a delimited
### per-type block. A NEW TYPE APPENDS A BLOCK; it does not edit a shared paragraph, and three
### branches landing at once therefore append in three places rather than colliding in one. If a
### thing is true of every type it belongs above this line instead.
###
### --- gofigure ---
###
### A goFigure puzzle, exactly as it arrives:
###
###   { "data": {
###       "acceptedSolutions": ["6+7+9*7", "6+9+7*7", "7+6+9*7", "7+9+6*7", "9+6+7*7", "9+7+6*7"],
###       "bank": [6, 9, 7, 7],
###       "goal": 154,
###       "hints": [
###         { "metadata": { "kind": "gofigure-operator", "operator": "+", "slot": 1 }, "text": "The 2nd operator from the left is \"+\"." },
###         { "metadata": { "kind": "gofigure-operator", "operator": "+", "slot": 0 }, "text": "The 1st operator from the left is \"+\"." },
###         { "metadata": { "kind": "gofigure-operator", "operator": "*", "slot": 2 }, "text": "The 3rd operator from the left is \"×\"." }
###       ],
###       "operators": ["+", "-", "*", "/"]
###     },
###     "difficulty": 4,
###     "estimatedSeconds": 150,
###     "id": "2026-08-20:gofigure:abc123de",
###     "type": "gofigure" }
###
### That is the original game's own puzzle and a real difficulty-4 board: one operator tuple (++*)
### across six expressions, which is what rates it a 4. The rung text above is byte-for-byte what
### buildHints emits for that solution list, glyphs included -- re-checked against a live buildHints
### call rather than trusted.
###
### THE DIFFICULTY IN THAT EXAMPLE IS ILLUSTRATIVE, and it is the one field not to read as a wire
### fact. 4 is a band goFigure can grade and can generate, but not one a pack ASKS for: the count
### table gives goFigure 1, 3 and 5, listed at the top of this file. The example stays at 4 because
### the unhedged wording below is the one-tuple case and this is the real board it was written
### against. Take the band set from the list at the top, never from this example.
###
### `hints` is REQUIRED and always exactly three rungs, so no read site needs to branch on its
### absence. Each rung names ONE operator slot of ONE canonical operator tuple, and the three are
### jointly satisfiable -- some accepted solution answers to all of them at once.
###
### goFigure's `metadata` carries `kind` plus TWO fields, and nothing derived from them:
###
###   `slot`     is the 0-based operator index, left to right, so `slot: 2` is the rightmost
###              operator, the one a player would call op3. What a board does with it is the board's
###              business -- there is no cell index here, because lull-ui renders the working
###              expression as one joined string and has no per-token cell.
###   `operator` is ASCII (+ - * /), matching `data.operators`, and is for MARKING THE BOARD, not for
###              printing. The rung's `text` already contains the same operator as the board glyph
###              (+ − × ÷ is U+002B, U+2212, U+00D7, U+00F7). The same fact in two alphabets is
###              deliberate: one is for reading, one is for the board.
###
### `kind` is the discriminator. It is `"gofigure-operator"` on every goFigure rung, and it is what
### a shared renderer switches on -- the presence of `operator` is NOT the tag. A second metadata
### shape arrives with its own `kind`; nothing in the type system enforces that, so it is a
### convention each type's golden ladder test pins for itself.
###
### The ladder ALWAYS ends on the rightmost operator, which is the strongest reveal: with the goal
### known it fixes the last step arithmetically, and on a * or / it names the final digit too. The
### first two rungs are ordered on the TUPLE COUNT, and on the one-tuple puzzles that order is
### deliberately NOT least-to-most-revealing -- rung 1 there reveals op2, which is why the example
### above opens on slot 1 and closes on slot 2. An ordinal in the text names the SLOT's position,
### never the rung's. Render the rungs in the order they arrive.
###
### THE WORDING, recorded here so a client can recognize it, NOT so a client can reproduce it. There
### are two bands, and which one a puzzle gets is decided here, from the number of distinct operator
### arrangements that reach the goal:
###
###   hedged, rung 1     One winning answer has "−" as its 1st operator.
###   hedged, rungs 2-3  The same answer has "×" as its 2nd operator.
###   unhedged, all      The 2nd operator from the left is "×".
###
### The hedge appears when more than one operator arrangement reaches the goal, because an
### unqualified claim about "the 1st operator" would then assert a uniqueness that does not hold.
### That is exactly the puzzles rated difficulty 1-3; the unhedged band is exactly 4 and 5. Do not
### key anything on that equivalence -- it is the backend's to spend, and it is spent already.
###
### "The same answer" is copy, not a claim that one expression was pinned -- a dozen expressions can
### share a tuple. It stops a reader taking the three rungs for three different solutions.
###
### The glyph is QUOTED in every rung. A bare U+2212 with a space either side is hard to tell from an
### em dash, so an unquoted rung reads as a sentence that broke off.
###
### DO NOT RENUMBER, and do not strip "from the left" out of the unhedged band. The rungs are ordered
### by how much each reveals, not left to right, so the one-tuple puzzles name their positions in the
### order 2, 1, 3. Anchoring the ordinal to the board is what stops it colliding with lull-ui's hint
### bar, which renders opened rungs into an ordered, decimal-marked list -- rung 1 would otherwise
### read "1. The 2nd operator is ×", two numbering systems disagreeing on one line. The hedged band
### needs no anchor: its ordinal hangs off "its", and its slots run 0, 1, 2 so marker and ordinal
### agree. That pairing holds because the 2, 1, 3 order and the unhedged copy are both the one-tuple
### case.
###
### `operators` is the PALETTE the board offers, always all four, and not the solution's operators.
### `acceptedSolutions` is every expression string reaching the goal, sorted, never empty, and
### evaluated strictly LEFT TO RIGHT with no operator precedence (6+7+9*7 is 154, not 76). Each bank
### digit is used exactly once. The device adjudicates locally against this list; the answer ships
### with the pack because the pack is already on the phone.
###
### There is no `operatorTuples` field. It used to ship the deduped operator arrangements so a client
### could decide the hedge itself; the sentence is written here now, so nothing reads it.
###
### --- end gofigure ---
###
### --- phrase-backed types: missingvowels, cryptogram ---
###
### ONE BLOCK RATHER THAN TWO, because these types are one phrase in two costumes and every rule
### below holds for both. A NEW PHRASE-BACKED TYPE APPENDS A LINE to the list immediately below and
### changes nothing else; a type with a `data` shape of its own opens its own block instead.
###
### Their rungs are text and nothing else:
###
###   missingvowels -> { "answer", "category"?, "displayed",
###                      "hints": [{ "text": "..." }, { "text": "..." }, { "text": "..." }] }
###   cryptogram    -> { "answer", "category"?, "ciphertext",
###                      "hints": [{ "text": "..." }, { "text": "..." }, { "text": "..." }] }
###
### No `metadata` key at all on those -- absent, not null. A phrase rung is a sentence; there is no
### structure on it for a board to act on.
###
### `answer` ships for the same offline-first reason `acceptedSolutions` does.
###
### `category` is OMITTED, not null, wherever it is hidden -- hiding it is part of the difficulty.
### The table hides it at difficulties 3 AND 5 (generators/category-visibility.ts), and BOTH ROWS ARE
### NOW LIVE. The forward-looking "today only difficulty 3 ever loses it" clause that stood here is
### STRUCK: Phrazle declares [3, 5], which is the first phrase type to reach band 5, so row 5 is a
### shape on the wire rather than a rule waiting for a type.
###
### What that means per type, since it falls out of the band sets rather than the table: Missing
### Vowels runs [1, 2] and always carries a category; Cryptogram runs [3, 4] and loses it at 3; and
### PHRAZLE RUNS [3, 5], SO IT NEVER CARRIES A CATEGORY ON EITHER OF ITS PUZZLES. That is deliberate
### rather than an accident of the table -- a category in a guessing game narrows the semantic space,
### and both of Phrazle's bands are hard ones.
###
### --- end phrase-backed types ---
###
### --- themedanagrams ---
###
### One theme and FOUR scrambled words, shown together and adjudicated PER WORD on the device. There
### is no top-level `answer` and there is no `category`, at any difficulty -- this type has four
### strings the player types, not one, and its label is `theme`.
###
###   { "data": {
###       "entries": [
###         { "answer": "KETTLE",   "scramble": "ELKTET" },
###         { "answer": "SAUCEPAN", "scramble": "UNASAPCE" },
###         { "answer": "SKILLET",  "scramble": "LKSETIL" },
###         { "answer": "SPATULA",  "scramble": "TPSLAAU" }
###       ],
###       "hints": [
###         { "metadata": { "entryIndex": 2, "kind": "themedanagrams-entry", "reveal": "initial" },
###           "text": "The 3rd answer starts with S." },
###         { "metadata": { "entryIndex": 3, "kind": "themedanagrams-entry", "reveal": "bookends" },
###           "text": "The 4th answer starts with S and ends with A." },
###         { "metadata": { "entryIndex": 1, "kind": "themedanagrams-entry", "reveal": "answer" },
###           "text": "The 2nd answer is SAUCEPAN." }
###       ],
###       "theme": "Kitchen tools" },
###     "difficulty": 3,
###     "estimatedSeconds": 90,
###     "id": "2026-09-02:themedanagrams:abc123de",
###     "type": "themedanagrams" }
###
### THE EXAMPLE'S WIRE ORDER IS DELIBERATELY NOT LENGTH-SORTED, so nothing about it suggests that a
### rung's position tells you which row it is about. Ranked by answer length it is SAUCEPAN, then
### SKILLET, then SPATULA (the tie broken by wire position), then KETTLE -- which is why the ordinals
### come out 3rd, 4th, 2nd and why KETTLE, the shortest, is never named.
###
### `entries` IS EXACTLY FOUR, and it is RENDERED IN WIRE ORDER. The rung ordinals index that order,
### so a board that sorts entries by length -- the obvious tidy-up -- makes every rung point at the
### wrong row. Do not sort them and do not renumber them.
###
### `answer` and `scramble` arrive as ONE OBJECT PER ENTRY, never as two parallel arrays. Both are
### uppercase A-Z, 5 to 9 letters, and the scramble is always the same length and the same letters as
### its own answer -- proved in code before the puzzle is written, so a board may rely on it.
###
### NEITHER `answer` NOR `scramble` IS A CHARGED TERM, and both halves of that are checked here rather
### than left to a client. The answer passes the whole-token blocklist in utils/charged-terms.ts, and
### its letters are additionally proved to spell no charged term at all -- the whole anagram class is
### absent from the lexicon a word is drawn from. The scramble is a string the backend COMPOSES, so it
### is checked again on the composed string at generate time and redrawn if it fails. A board does not
### filter this and must not: filtering on the device would mean two different lists, and the one on
### the device would be the stale one.
###
### `answer` ships for the same offline-first reason `acceptedSolutions` does: the device adjudicates
### locally and the pack is already on it. Compare with the shared normalizer, as every type does.
###
### `theme` IS ALWAYS SHOWN, at every difficulty. It is never hidden and it is never omitted -- hiding
### it would turn a one-answer puzzle into a several-answer one, which is a different game. It is at
### most 40 characters and at most four words.
###
### THE HINT METADATA, which is this type's own member of the shared `kind` union:
###
###   "kind"        always "themedanagrams-entry".
###   "entryIndex"  0-BASED, an index into `entries`. THE ORDINAL IN `text` IS entryIndex + 1: they
###                 are the same row expressed two ways. A client that treats this field as 1-based
###                 highlights the wrong row while printing the right sentence.
###   "reveal"      "initial" | "bookends" | "answer" -- WHAT KIND of reveal the rung is, never the
###                 revealed letters. The letters are already on the wire in
###                 entries[entryIndex].answer; slice them yourself. A second copy in the metadata
###                 would be an independent input describing the same fact, and the two could
###                 disagree.
###
### The three rungs are always about THREE DIFFERENT entries, escalating in kind rather than in
### strength on one row: first letter, then first and last letter, then the whole answer. They are
### code-written from a closed set of templates, they never name the theme, and they are under 80
### characters. Render `text` verbatim, as on every other type.
###
### The SHORTEST entry is never named by any rung, and the strongest reveal lands on the LONGEST. That
### is a backend decision and not something to re-derive on the board.
###
### --- end themedanagrams ---
