### --- crypticclue ---
###
### { "data": {
###     "answer": "TANGO",
###     "clue": "Dance hidden in instant angora",
###     "definitionSpan": { "end": 5, "start": 0 },
###     "device": "hidden",
###     "enumeration": [5],
###     "fodderSpan": { "end": 30, "start": 16 },
###     "hints": [
###       { "text": "The wordplay is a hidden word: the answer's letters sit consecutively inside the clue, spanning a word break." },
###       { "text": "The definition is \"Dance\"." },
###       { "text": "Five letters, beginning with T." } ] },
###   "difficulty": 3,
###   "estimatedSeconds": 120,
###   "id": "2026-10-01:crypticclue:abcd1234",
###   "type": "crypticclue" }
###
### `clue`            the surface reading, rendered VERBATIM. Letters and single spaces only, at most
###                   120 characters. It carries NO enumeration parenthetical -- the client renders
###                   `clue`, then ` (${enumeration.join(',')})`.
### `answer`          the solution, ONE uppercase word of 4-8 letters. Adjudicate through the shared
###                   normalizeAnswer rule, as on every other type.
### `enumeration`     word lengths, derived in code from `answer`, so it cannot disagree with it.
###                   Always length 1 today; the field is an array because the WIRE SHAPE is the
###                   expensive thing to change.
### `device`          "hidden" or "anagram" -- what the wordplay does. Rung 1 already says it in
###                   words; this is the machine-readable form for a post-solve explanation.
### `definitionSpan`  HALF-OPEN [start, end) UTF-16 CODE-UNIT offsets into `clue`. The clue's charset
### `fodderSpan`      admits no non-ASCII character, so code unit, code point and grapheme coincide --
###                   but slice by CODE UNIT anyway, because that is what the numbers are. Both are
###                   computed in code by locating the model's part strings and then discarding them;
###                   the model never returns an offset, because a model that miscounts one character
###                   would ship a hint quoting the wrong words.
###
### THERE IS NO indicatorSpan. The `device` literal already names what the indicator signals, and a
### field with no reader is a field that rots.
###
### `category` is ABSENT BY DESIGN rather than hidden: the definition half IS the category, it is
### always on the wire because it is inside the clue, and hiding it is not available. Do not branch
### on its absence.
###
### THE SPANS INDEX `clue` BYTE FOR BYTE. Do not trim it, do not collapse its whitespace, do not
### re-encode it before slicing: every one of those silently moves both spans while they still
### produce a rendering. The API stores it identical to the string it verified, which is why a clue
### needing a trim is rejected rather than trimmed.
###
### BEST EFFORT. This type is excluded from `complete`, so a pack that shipped no cryptic clue is
### still complete and the client must NOT treat a missing crypticclue puzzle as a reason to refetch.
### Render what arrives.
###
### --- end crypticclue ---
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
